WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.060
Once, the United States was filled with beavers.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.500
Up to 250 million beaver ponds

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.180
once puddled the continent — that’s enough water to

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.340
submerge Washington, Oregon, and California.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.220
But by the beginning of the 20th century,

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.220
after decades of over trapping by fur traders,

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.410
just 100,000 beavers survived.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.060
The slaughter transformed North America’s waterways.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:24.940
Beaver activity

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.660
-- building dams and forming ponds and wetlands --

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.420
slows the flow of water, captures sediment,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.520
and reduces erosion,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.080
After beavers and their speed bumps disappeared,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.960
thousands of streams straightened

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.500
and deepened into gullies.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.200
Many streams stopped spilling onto their floodplains

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.220
preventing them from recharging water tables.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.640
Some streams dried up altogether.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.260
Now researchers are attempting to “re-beaver”

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.360
these run-down valleys with Beaver Dam Analogs

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.400
or BDAs.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.940
A row of peeled logs form its frame;

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.400
a lattice of woven willow sticks comprise its body.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.020
BDAs provide powerful recruitment tools,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.600
starter kits that offer similar services as

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.240
natural beaver dams

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.720
while also helping the paddle-tailed engineers

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.400
settle where they’re most needed.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.980
Between 2009 and 2012, researchers installed

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.900
121 Beaver Dam Analogues along Bridge Creek,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.760
a 45-kilometer long stream

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.440
within Oregon’s Columbia River basin.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.540
The goal was to convert a drastically simplified stream

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.280
into a complex one.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.280
Sure enough, wherever they put structures,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.920
beavers came and set up shop,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.880
By 2013, beavers had built 115 new dams

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.060
and fortified nearly 60 BDAs.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.340
And with the beavers, came results -

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.300
as the streambed rose, water coursed once more

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.680
on to the floodplain, tripling the creek’s

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.020
submerged area and increasing side channels

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.520
by over 1200 percent.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:56.540
In Wyoming,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.500
BDAs are creating wet meadows for sage grouse;

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.740
in Utah, they’re sub-irrigating cattle pastures;

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.280
in Oregon, they’re rebuilding eroded salmon streams.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.760
For all that momentum, however,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.940
Beaver Dam Analogs continue to hit snags.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.880
Many landowners, as well as some agencies,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.840
still consider beavers tree-felling, field-flooding,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.240
ditch-clogging nuisances, and look askance

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.380
on any effort that invites their colonization.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.860
Last year alone, the U.S. Department of Agriculture

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.100
trapped more than 23,000 beavers.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.920
But the idea is slowly taking hold -

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.980
ranchers in California’s Scott Valley Watershed are

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.600
warming up to beavers and their human imitators,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.760
persuaded by water tables that have risen

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.680
by as much as three feet and reduced irrigation costs.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.060
Happily, beavers have returned to countless

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.260
North American waterways to lend a helping hand...

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.100
or paw.

